// Original teaching examples; technical labels follow the user's starting list.
const rows = [
['chiasmus','Turn the relationship around','Reverse the order of two ideas or grammatical structures so the second half reframes the first.','We shape our habits; our habits shape us.','Let your work serve your values, not your values serve your work.','Write a sentence about time and money. Reverse their roles to reveal a priority.','Does the reversal reveal a real difference, or merely sound clever?'],
['anaphora','Give each thought the same beginning','Repeat an opening phrase across successive clauses. Keep the opening steady while the thought develops.','We need room to learn. We need room to fail. We need room to begin again.','Let the doors open for the curious. Let the doors open for the uncertain.','Write three reasons to support a cause, beginning each with the same words.','Does each repetition add something? Let the final one carry the most weight.'],
['epistrophe','Bring every line home','Repeat the same ending across clauses so different paths arrive at one shared point.','We planned for our neighbors, built for our neighbors, and stayed for our neighbors.','The meeting belongs to everyone. The decision belongs to everyone.','Describe three different efforts and end each with the same purpose.','Does the repeated ending deserve the emphasis it receives?'],
['anadiplosis','Build a chain of thought','Take the last important word of one clause and make it the start of the next.','Listening creates trust. Trust makes cooperation possible.','Practice produces confidence; confidence invites experimentation.','Build a three-link chain from a small action to a larger result.','Can you explain the causal link between each pair?'],
['alliteration','Make a phrase feel connected','Place nearby words with matching initial consonant sounds. Read aloud to hear the pattern.','Patient people protect public places.','Small steps strengthen shaky starts.','Describe a worthwhile action in a short phrase with two or three matching initial sounds.','Is it easy to say naturally, without becoming a tongue twister?'],
['anacoluthon','Let a thought break its frame','Begin with one sentence structure and shift into another, conveying interruption or urgency.','If we had only listened—but there is still time.','The people who waited outside—how could we forget them?','Start a measured statement, then let an urgent thought interrupt its grammar.','Can a listener follow the shift without rereading?'],
['analepsis','Return to an earlier moment','Interrupt the present sequence to show an earlier event that changes how we understand it.','Today the hall is full. Two winters ago, we met here with three chairs and no heat.','Before I describe our first success, remember the night we almost stopped.','Open in the present, step back to a revealing moment, then return to now.','Does the earlier scene change the meaning of the present?'],
['antanaclasis','Repeat a word, change its meaning','Use the same word twice in different senses. Make the change in meaning carry the point.','We cannot afford to lose interest in the interest we pay.','A fair price is not enough to make a trade fair.','Choose a word with two meanings, such as interest, fair, or change. Connect both in one argument.','Are both meanings clear on a first hearing?'],
['antiphrasis','Say the opposite with intent','Use a word ironically, relying on context to make its opposite meaning unmistakable.','A brilliant shortcut: it added an hour to the trip.','What a generous welcome—every door was locked.','Describe a small everyday failure with an obviously contrary word of praise.','Would your audience recognize the irony without seeing your expression?'],
['antonomasia','Let a name become a description','Replace a name with a recognizable title, or use a familiar name as a type of person.','Our neighborhood peacemaker settled the dispute before lunch.','Every workshop needs a Sherlock to find the missing screw.','Give a person a descriptive title that makes a relevant quality memorable.','Does the audience know the reference, and is the comparison fair?'],
['apophasis','Mention it by setting it aside','Bring up a point while saying you will not dwell on it. The apparent omission still draws attention.','I will not dwell on the empty chairs; let us discuss who was invited.','Leaving aside the three missed deadlines, what has changed in the plan?','Write a transition that briefly acknowledges a relevant problem before moving to your main case.','Are you introducing a fair point, or smuggling in an accusation you will not defend?'],
['aporia','Think through doubt aloud','Express uncertainty to invite the audience into a genuine difficulty.','Should we act quickly, or wait until everyone has been heard?','I do not know which costs more: the risk of trying, or the certainty of standing still.','State a real tension in your topic as a question you cannot dismiss easily.','Does the uncertainty open inquiry instead of avoiding a decision?'],
['cacophony','Make friction audible','Cluster rough, clashing sounds so the sound of the sentence reinforces discomfort or force.','Carts clattered across cracked tracks.','The gates scraped, clanked, and slammed shut.','Describe an obstacle with consonants that make the sentence feel rough aloud.','Does the sound serve the scene without making the sentence hard to understand?'],
['dialogism','Expose the either-or implication','From a premise, draw a conclusion with two alternatives. This is the logical sense used in the starting list.','This unpaid work creates value. Either pay is not the measure of value, or our measure is incomplete.','The rule excludes a qualified applicant. Either the rule needs changing, or qualification is not its aim.','Choose a premise that challenges a rule. State the two alternatives it forces us to consider.','Do the alternatives actually follow, or have you invented a false dilemma?'],
['dysphemism','Choose the harsher frame','Substitute a disparaging expression for a neutral one, making the judgment audible.','A filing system becomes a paperwork swamp.','An unused office becomes a storage tomb.','Describe the same inconvenience neutrally, then with a harsher image.','What judgment did your choice add? Could you defend it?'],
['epizeuxis','Repeat without a pause in the thought','Repeat a word immediately to intensify a moment.','Enough, enough. Let us hear their answer.','Now, now is the moment to begin.','Write a turning point with one immediate repetition, then continue the argument.','Does the moment warrant that intensity?'],
['hypallage','Move the description','Transfer a description from the person or thing it logically belongs to onto something associated with it.','We spent a sleepless night beside the phone.','She walked a worried mile to the station.','Describe waiting by moving the person’s feeling onto the time or place.','Can the listener still tell who really feels the emotion?'],
['hyperbaton','Change the expected word order','Move words away from their usual positions to give a particular idea emphasis.','This much we can promise.','A better answer we have yet to hear.','Write an ordinary sentence, then move its most important phrase to the front.','Does the rearrangement sound deliberate rather than confusing?'],
['hyperbole','Exaggerate to reveal intensity','Overstate something recognizably to convey feeling or scale.','I have carried this question through a thousand meetings.','That tiny delay swallowed the whole afternoon.','Exaggerate a familiar frustration, then write its literal equivalent.','Will listeners understand this as emphasis rather than factual evidence?'],
['hypophora','Ask, then answer','Raise a question or objection and answer it yourself, guiding the audience through a step in the argument.','Will this take time? Yes—and less time than repairing the same mistake again.','What would change tomorrow? The doors would open an hour earlier.','Ask the strongest practical question about your proposal. Answer it directly.','Did you choose the audience’s real question, or an easy one?'],
['litotes','Affirm through a negative','Express a positive judgment by denying its opposite.','That is no small achievement.','Her answer was not without courage.','Praise an achievement by denying an opposite quality, then compare it with direct praise.','Does the restraint strengthen the praise or make it sound reluctant?'],
['meiosis','Make restraint carry the weight','Understate the size or importance of something, allowing the context to supply the force.','After rebuilding the entire hall: We made a few repairs.','After an all-night rescue: It was a busy evening.','Describe a major effort with a modest phrase, and supply enough context to reveal the contrast.','Will the audience understand the true scale?'],
['metaphor','Give an idea another form','Describe one thing as another to carry a useful resemblance into the argument.','Trust is the foundation; promises are only the paint.','This policy is a bridge with no far bank.','Describe an abstract problem as a physical object. Develop one useful implication.','Which part of the comparison explains something, and where does it stop working?'],
['metonymy','Name the thing beside it','Refer to something through a closely associated object, place, or symbol.','The town hall has finally listened.','The microphone must make room for quieter voices.','Replace an institution or role with a familiar associated object or place.','Will your audience identify the intended people or institution?'],
['onomatopoeia','Let the sound enter the sentence','Use words whose sounds imitate what they describe.','The latch clicked. The room went quiet.','Rain hissed against the windows as the vote began.','Set a scene using two specific sounds, then connect the scene to your point.','Does the sound make the scene concrete rather than decorative?'],
['oxymoron','Put opposites together','Join apparently contradictory terms to express a tension in one compact phrase.','The room settled into a restless calm.','Their agreement was an uneasy peace.','Name a situation with two conflicting qualities, then explain why both are true.','Is there a meaningful tension behind the phrase?'],
['pleonasm','Use deliberate extra words','Add words that repeat meaning to stress a point or create a spoken rhythm.','I heard it with my own ears.','We will build it together, all of us.','Write a concise claim, then repeat part of its meaning for emphasis.','Does the added wording earn its space when spoken?'],
['simile','Make the comparison explicit','Compare unlike things with like or as, helping a listener see a shared quality.','A promise without a plan is like a map without roads.','The rumor spread like ink through water.','Compare a difficult idea to an everyday experience your audience knows.','Does the comparison clarify the specific quality you mean?'],
['syllepsis','Let one verb do two jobs','Let one governing word apply to two objects, one literally and one figuratively. This overlaps with zeugma.','She carried a suitcase and a secret.','He lost his keys and his confidence.','Pair a concrete object and an abstract one under a single verb.','Does the verb make sense in both uses, and does the pairing add meaning?'],
['synecdoche','Let a part stand for the whole','Name a whole through one of its parts, or a part through the whole.','We need more hands in the garden.','Every roof in this valley deserves protection.','Represent a group or place through a concrete part of it.','Does the part bring the whole into focus without erasing the people involved?'],
['zeugma','Join unexpected partners','Use one governing word across different objects or senses for a compact surprise.','She opened the window and the discussion.','They broke the silence and the agreement.','Find a verb that can connect two unexpected objects. Write a line where the connection supports your point.','Does the surprise sharpen the thought? This family overlaps with syllepsis.']
];
export const devices = rows.map(([id,title,mechanism,example,example2,prompt,check])=>({id,title,mechanism,examples:[example,example2],prompt,check}));
